# Can rational choice explain crime?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "What did Becker's economics of crime claim? What does the evidence say about deterrence?"
next: {"title":"Can economics explain marriage and children?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/94-can-economics-explain-marriage-and.cast"}

## One day older
sources:
    - id: lm
      title: 'The Deterrence Effect of Prison: Dynamic Theory and Evidence'
      authors: Lee, D. S. & McCrary, J.
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Florida arrest data: the probability of a prison sentence and the expected sentence jump at the eighteenth birthday (adult expected sentence more than twice the juvenile one), yet offending falls very little; implied elasticity of crime with respect to sentence length about −0.05 (as summarised by Chalfin & McCrary 2017).'
    - id: becker1968
      title: 'Crime and Punishment: An Economic Approach'
      authors: Becker, G. S.
      year: 1968
      finding: A rational person offends when the expected utility of the crime exceeds that of abstaining; the expected cost depends on the probability of conviction p and the punishment f.
      doi: 10.1086/259394
    - id: becker1993
      title: 'Nobel Lecture: The Economic Way of Looking at Behavior'
      authors: Becker, G. S.
      year: 1993
      finding: Becker says he began thinking about crime after weighing a paid lot against the risk of a parking ticket on the way to an oral exam at Columbia.
    - id: cm2017
      title: 'Criminal Deterrence: A Review of the Literature'
      authors: Chalfin, A. & McCrary, J.
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Summarises Lee & McCrary: at 18, Florida offenders face an expected sentence more than twice the juvenile one, yet offending falls very little (elasticity about −0.05).'
      doi: 10.1257/jel.20141147

