    card title "One day older"

Can you deter crime the way a price deters a buyer, by making it cost more?
    draw teen dayline

In Florida, (@draw d17 cut d18 cake@) a young offender caught the day before his eighteenth birthday is usually dealt with as a juvenile.

B: Caug (@draw bartitle bar_juv lab_juv@)ht the day after, he's an adult, and the se (@draw bar_adult lab_adult times2@)ntence he can expect more than doubles.

Same person, same crime, one day apart. Only the punishment jumps.
    move teen by [280,0] duration 1.5

B: My bet: a big drop in offending on the birthday. Teenagers can count.
    highlight times2

    quiz "On the eighteenth birthday, how much do young offenders cut back?" judge false
        right: Hold that guess: the data come once we have the model.
        * By about half
        * By about a quarter
        * Barely at all

    erase teen dayline cut d17 d18 cake bartitle bar_juv bar_adult lab_juv lab_adult times2

The idea that they should is Gary Becker's, from a 1968 paper called 'Crime and Punishment: An Economic Approach'.
    draw becker paper

    pause 0.3

    erase paper

B: The parking-ticket sum again, gain against expected fine, now for crime.
    draw parking

    erase becker parking

Someone offends when the gain beats the expected punishment:
    draw m_g c_g m_gt

the chance of being caught times the punishment.
    draw m_p c_p m_x m_f c_f

B: No special criminal mind, then. Ordinary people, facing different prices.
    highlight rule effect box

So crime has two dials, certainty and severity,
    text k_p "certainty" above m_p gap 30 #2f6b8f font_size 38
    text k_f "severity" above m_f gap 30 #b5482e font_size 38
    parallel true

and the eighteenth birthday turns only one of them.
    highlight k_f effect circle

## Two dials
domain: {"x":[0,1],"y":[0,20]}
vars:
    p:
      value: 0.1
      min: 0.02
      max: 1
      step: 0.01
    f:
      value: 5
      min: 0
      max: 20
      step: 0.25
    E:
      expr: p*f*1000
    q:
      value: 0.5
sources:
    - id: becker1968
      title: 'Crime and Punishment: An Economic Approach'
      authors: Becker, G. S.
      year: 1968
      finding: An offence pays when its gain exceeds the expected punishment p·f. Since raising f is cheap and raising p costs police, the model pushes towards severe, rarely imposed punishment; if offenders are risk preferrers, p is the stronger deterrent, if risk averse, f is.
      doi: 10.1086/259394
    - id: cm2017
      title: 'Criminal Deterrence: A Review of the Literature'
      authors: Chalfin, A. & McCrary, J.
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Restates Becker: p is more effective than f if and only if offenders are risk preferring; equally effective if risk neutral.'
      doi: 10.1257/jel.20141147

    axes ax x_label "chance caught, p" y_label "punishment f ($1,000s)" cross corner ticks.x [0.2,0.4,0.6] ticks.y [5,10,15,20] hidden true
    icon motor at.data [0.62,17.5] size 80 or ["speedboat","boat"] icon_look picture of "motor boat" hidden true
    text l_motor "gain g = $1,000" #c99a1e at.data [0.83,17.5] font_size 34 hidden true
    dot thief #3d3833 at.x 0.1 at.y 5 bind {"at.x":"p","at.y":"f"} hidden true
    label l_thief "p × f = ${E:0,}" right attach_to thief font_size 34 hidden true
    curve ceil expr "20" x_from 0.05 x_to 1 style.opacity 0 hidden true
    curve deter thick #b5482e expr 1/x x_from 0.05 x_to 1 hidden true
    region zone x_from 0.05 x_to 1 between ["deter","ceil"] style.fill #87a878 style.opacity 0.18 hidden true
    text l_zone "deterred" #5d7d50 at.data [0.55,11] font_size 40 hidden true
    text l_deter "deterrence line" #b5482e at.data [0.82,3.3] font_size 34 hidden true
    dot m_half #b5482e at.x 0.5 at.y 2 hidden true
    label l_half "½ × $2,000" above-right attach_to m_half font_size 30 hidden true
    dot m_tenth #b5482e at.x 0.1 at.y 10 hidden true
    label l_tenth "1/10 × $10,000" above-right attach_to m_tenth font_size 30 hidden true
    dot slide #3d3833 at.x 0.5 at.y 2 bind {"at.x":"q","at.y":"1/q"} hidden true
    text c_police "police: costly →" #2f6b8f at.data [0.45,6.5] font_size 32 hidden true
    text c_fines "↑ fines: cheap" #b5482e at.data [0.17,19] font_size 32 hidden true
    text risk "likes risk → p\ndislikes risk → f" at.data [0.68,14] font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after hidden true
    scratch mathcard top-right work [{"tex":"\\text{deterred if } p\\,f \\ge g"},{"tex":"\\text{line: } f = g / p"},{"tex":"1.1p \\cdot f = p \\cdot 1.1f = 1.1\\,pf"}] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Two dials"

Becker's rule has two dials, so give each one an axis: the chance of being caught across, the punishment up.
    draw ax

B: In Harbourtown, say, a thi (@draw motor l_motor@)ef eyes an outboard motor at the boatyard. Selling it gains him a thousand dollars: illustrative numbers.

B: Put the punishment in dollars too: a fine, or what a spell in prison is worth to him in money.
    highlight ax

So a one-in- (@draw thief@)ten chance of a five-thousand-dollar punishment.

B: Expected punishment: five hundred dollars. Less than the motor, so the motor goes.
    draw l_thief

Where does the theft stop paying?
    highlight l_thief l_motor

Wherever p times f reaches a thousand: one in two with two thousand,
    draw m_half l_half

one in ten with ten thousand.
    draw m_tenth l_tenth

Join every such point, and that's the deterrence line.
    draw deter l_deter ceil zone l_zone

    erase l_half l_tenth m_tenth

Try it: slide the chance and the punishment, and find two different ways across the line.
    explore params ["p","f"]

B: Every point on the line deters equally. But they don't cost the same. Catching more thieves takes police;
    draw c_police

B: a bigger fine costs almost nothing.
    draw c_fines

    hide thief l_thief

    show slide

So Becker's own logic says: catch few, punish hard. Slide up the line, toward the corner.
    animate q 0.05 duration 3.5 ghost true

    erase c_police c_fines

B: With a twist Becker spotted himself. If offenders like a gamble, the chance matters more than the size; if they dislike risk, the size matters more.
    draw risk

So the model can't tell us which dial is stronger. Only evidence can.
    highlight deter

    erase risk

    draw mathq

    ask "Want the math behind the deterrence line?" on mathq store mq default "Skip it" stage own

@math

    erase mathq

In symbols: the theft is deterred when p times f is at least g.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

So the line is f equals g over p: halve the chance, double the punishment.
    draw mathcard_line_2

And ten percent more of either dial raises p times f by the same ten percent. Only attitudes to risk break the tie.
    draw mathcard_line_3

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

## Which dial works?
use: bar_chart
with:
    labels:
      - 'Police: violent'
      - 'Police: property'
      - 3 strikes
      - Age 18
    values:
      - 4
      - 2
      - 0.6
      - 0.5
    value_labels: true
    value_format:
      suffix: '%'
      decimals: 1
    colors:
      - green
      - green
      - red
      - red
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 5
    y_label: Less crime per 10% more
    label_size: 32
    gap: 0.5
sources:
    - id: levitt1997
      title: Using Electoral Cycles in Police Hiring to Estimate the Effect of Police on Crime
      authors: Levitt, S. D.
      year: 1997
      finding: Police hiring rises before mayoral and gubernatorial elections; used as an instrument, it gives large negative effects of police on (especially violent) crime.
      doi: 10.1257/aer.87.3.270
    - id: mccrary2002
      title: 'Using Electoral Cycles in Police Hiring to Estimate the Effect of Police on Crime: Comment'
      authors: McCrary, J.
      year: 2002
      finding: A weighting error in Levitt's estimation; corrected, the electoral-cycle estimates are not statistically significant (the first stage is weak).
      doi: 10.1257/00028280260344803
    - id: cm2017
      title: 'Criminal Deterrence: A Review of the Literature'
      authors: Chalfin, A. & McCrary, J.
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Best guess: elasticity of violent crime w.r.t. police about −0.4, property crime about −0.2; far less evidence that crime responds to sanction severity. Helland & Tabarrok''s estimates imply a sentence-length elasticity of about −0.06; Lee & McCrary''s about −0.05.'
      doi: 10.1257/jel.20141147
    - id: ht2007
      title: Does Three Strikes Deter? A Nonparametric Estimation
      authors: Helland, E. & Tabarrok, A.
      year: 2007
      finding: California's three-strikes law cut felony arrest rates of offenders with two strikes by about 17–20 percent (two-strike convicts vs. those tried for a strike but convicted of a lesser offence).
      url: https://ideas.repec.org/a/uwp/jhriss/v42y2007i2p309-330.html
    - id: lm
      title: 'The Deterrence Effect of Prison: Dynamic Theory and Evidence'
      authors: Lee, D. S. & McCrary, J.
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Florida: the expected sentence more than doubles at age 18, yet arrests barely fall; implied elasticity of crime w.r.t. sentence length about −0.05.'

    icon cop size 80 x 860 y 560 icon_look picture of "police officer" hidden true
    text l_cycle "hired before elections" cites ["levitt1997"] x 780 y 480 font_size 32 hidden true
    mark strike around cop l_cycle kind strike hidden true
    text l_err "weighting error" #b5482e cites ["mccrary2002"] x 780 y 430 font_size 32 hidden true
    text l_ht "two strikes: 17–20% fewer arrests" #b5482e cites ["ht2007"] x 640 y 520 font_size 32 hidden true
    text l_cert "certainty" #5d7d50 x 360 y 560 font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Which dial works?"

So the data must decide. Start with the chance of being caught: more police.
    draw axes

B: Hard to test. Cities with more crime hire more police, so police and crime rise together.
    draw cop

In 1997 Steven Levitt found a way ro (@draw l_cycle@)und it: mayors and governors hire extra police before elections, crime or no crime.

B: Then Justin McCrary found an error i (@draw strike l_err@)n the weighting. Corrected, the estimates were no longer statistically significant.

    erase cop l_cycle strike l_err

Later studies found other natural experiments. Chalfin and McCrary's 2017 revi (@draw bar_1 bar_2@)ew sums them up: ten percent more police, about four percent less violent crime and two percent less property crime.

B: Now the other dial. California's three-strikes law: a third felony, after two serious ones, means twenty-five years to life.
    text l_sev "severity" #b5482e x 770 y 600 font_size 38

Eric Helland and Alex Tabarrok found that offenders with two strikes were arrested seventeen to twenty percent less often.
    draw l_ht

    erase l_ht

B: Real, but for an enormous jump in sentence.  (@draw bar_3@)Per ten percent longer, about six-tenths of a percent less crime.

    ask "Drag the age-18 bar to your guess: how much less crime per 10% longer sentence?" on bar_4 store g default "2.5" gap false
        intro: And the eighteenth birthday, where the expected sentence more than doubles?
        right: About half a percent: close to your {g}.
        wrong: You said {g}: it's about half a percent.

B: Barely at all, as David Lee and Justin McCrary found: about half a percent less crime per ten percent longer sentence.
    highlight bar_4

The ce (@highlight bar_1 bar_2@)rtain dial beats the severe one, by a wide margin.
    draw l_cert

## Impatient, or not choosing?
sources:
    - id: cm2017
      title: 'Criminal Deterrence: A Review of the Literature'
      authors: Chalfin, A. & McCrary, J.
      year: 2017
      finding: 'Considerable evidence that crime responds to police; far less that it responds to sanction severity. Lee and McCrary''s dynamic model: impatient offenders respond much less to sentence length, since most of a prison term''s cost lies in the future.'
      doi: 10.1257/jel.20141147
    - id: nagin2013
      title: Deterrence in the Twenty-First Century
      authors: Nagin, D. S.
      year: 2013
      finding: The certainty of apprehension is a far more effective deterrent than the severity of punishment (summarised by NIJ, 'Five Things About Deterrence', 2016).
    - id: merton1938
      title: Social Structure and Anomie
      authors: Merton, R. K.
      year: 1938
      finding: Deviance arises where culture holds out goals to all but the social structure restricts legitimate means of reaching them (strain theory).
      doi: 10.2307/2084686
    - id: sutherland
      title: Principles of Criminology (differential association)
      authors: Sutherland, E. H.
      year: 1947
      finding: Criminal behaviour is learned in interaction with others, in intimate personal groups.

    math m_g tex g size 100 colors.g #c99a1e x 300 y 560 hidden true
    math m_gt tex > size 100 x 400 y 560 hidden true
    math m_p tex p size 100 colors.p #2f6b8f x 500 y 560 hidden true
    math m_x tex \times size 100 x 600 y 560 hidden true
    math m_f tex f size 100 colors.f #b5482e x 700 y 560 hidden true
    group rule members ["m_g","m_gt","m_p","m_x","m_f"] hidden true
    text c_p "tonight" below m_p gap 25 #2f6b8f font_size 34 hidden true
    text c_f "years later" below m_f gap 25 #b5482e font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Impatient, or not choosing?"

B: So offenders shrug at longer sentences but notice police. Doesn't that sink the rational offender?
    draw m_g m_gt m_p m_x m_f

Not (@draw c_f@) quite. (@draw c_p@) Being caught happens tonight; prison comes years later.

Someone with strong present bias barely counts a cost that far off.
    move m_f scale 0.5 duration 1.5

B: Rational, but impatient. Or not really choosing at all: a fight outside a bar is not a calculation.
    box n_imp "Impatience" width 290 height 130 radius 12 icon hourglass font_size 32 in views

Sociologists start further back: not the moment of choice, but what shapes it.
    highlight views

B: Robert Merton's strain theory: crime grows where the goals are open to all but the legal routes are not.
    box n_strain "Strain: Merton 1938" cites ["merton1938"] width 290 height 130 radius 12 icon ladder font_size 32 in views

B: And Edwin Sutherland's: crime is learned from the people around you.
    box n_learn "Learning: Sutherland" cites ["sutherland"] width 290 height 130 radius 12 icon people font_size 32 in views
    row views gap 40 fit.h 220 fit.w 920 fit.x 40 fit.y 230 hidden true

Becker's economic imperialism, carried onto criminology's ground. Here the visitors learned from the locals.
    text imperial "economic imperialism?" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 190 font_size 36

    erase imperial views

How sure are we? That certainty deters more than severity: mostly agreed, as Daniel Nagin's survey of the research concludes.
    text st_cert "Certainty > severity: mostly agreed" #2f6b8f cites ["nagin2013","cm2017"] x 500 y 360 font_size 42

B: That crime is mainly a rational choice: contested. People respond at the margin; who stands at the margin is a sociologist's question too.
    text st_choice "Crime as mainly choice: contested" #b5482e x 500 y 270 font_size 42

    quiz "A speed camera fines every speeder $100; a patrol fines one speeder in ten $1,000. Same expected fine. Which likely cuts speeding more?"
        intro: Three questions to try it on new cases.
        right: The camera: the same expected fine, but certain. Theory alone can't decide this, but the evidence says certainty is what people notice.
        wrong: Which dial does the evidence say people notice more? Here, the camera.
        + The camera
        * The patrol
        * No difference

    quiz "A town doubles every fine but cuts patrols, so the chance of being caught halves. In Becker's model, what happens to the expected punishment?" voice b
        right: It stays the same: half the chance times twice the fine. On the evidence, crime would more likely rise than fall.
        wrong: Expected punishment is chance times punishment: here, it stays the same.
        * It doubles
        + It stays the same
        * It halves

    quiz "A shop plagued by teenage shoplifters can post a visible guard at the door, or announce that every case caught will be prosecuted, with a court date months later. Which fits the evidence better?"
        right: The visible guard: a likely catch today counts for more than a court date months away.
        wrong: Impatient offenders barely count a cost months away: here, the visible guard.
        + The visible guard
        * The prosecution policy
        * Neither can work

So can rational choice explain crime? Part of it: offenders respond to the odds they can see tonight, far less to punishments years away.
    highlight m_p

The cheapest deterrent is a likelier catch, not a longer sentence.
    highlight c_p

